<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Supplemental Material</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Charge yield of <math id="A1.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 124$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>Xe</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"></mpadded><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"><mn mathsize="0.700em">124</mn></mpadded></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 124$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}</annotation></semantics></math> double-electron capture events</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ECEC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 124$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>Xe</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"></mpadded><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"><mn mathsize="0.700em">124</mn></mpadded></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 124$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes to the ER background in the WIMP ROI with an expected number of (<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.5\pm 0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.5\pm 0.7</annotation></semantics></math>) events in SR0 and (<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.1\pm 1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>1.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.1\pm 1.4</annotation></semantics></math>) events in SR1 from the LM + LN (<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5.m1" class="ltx_markedasmath" alttext="\sim 6\text{\,}\mathrm{keV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">keV</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 6\text{\,}\mathrm{keV}</annotation></semantics></math>) and LL (<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6.m1" class="ltx_markedasmath" alttext="\sim 10\text{\,}\mathrm{keV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">keV</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 10\text{\,}\mathrm{keV}</annotation></semantics></math>) peaks.
The event rate was constrained via the KK and KL-shell capture peaks outside the ROI, using the branching fractions 72.4 % (KK), 20.0 % (KL), 1.4 % (LL), and 0.7 % (LM + LN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
The de-excitation cascade following the capture can lead to an increased ionization density, which may result in a higher recombination probability and thus lower <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CY</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CY}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upbeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upbeta</annotation></semantics></math> decay events.
A charge suppression factor of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 0.9</annotation></semantics></math> was measured for L-shell electron capture in <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 127$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>Xe</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"></mpadded><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"><mn mathsize="0.700em">127</mn></mpadded></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 127$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11.m1" class="ltx_markedasmath" alttext="\sim 5.2\text{\,}\mathrm{keV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>5.2</mn></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">keV</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 5.2\text{\,}\mathrm{keV}</annotation></semantics></math>) at drift fields above 250 V/cm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>,
but no such measurement exists for <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ECEC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}</annotation></semantics></math> at the 23 V/cm field used in this search.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Introducing reduced charge yields as nuisance parameters could bias upper limits by absorbing leakage from other ER backgrounds, artificially lowering them.
Thus, we first tested the hypothesis of a reduced <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CY</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CY}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ECEC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}</annotation></semantics></math> peaks at 23 V/cm using SR1 science data.
For this, an alternative likelihood function was defined, modeling the <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ECEC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}</annotation></semantics></math> peaks as separate components with <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LL}}=\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}_{LL}}/\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}}_{\upbeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LL</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CY</mi><mi>LL</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>CY</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">β</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LL}}=\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}_{LL}}/\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}}_{\upbeta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LM}}=\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}_{LM}}/\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}}_{\upbeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LM</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CY</mi><mi>LM</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>CY</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">β</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LM}}=\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}_{LM}}/\mathrm{\mathrm{CY}}_{\upbeta}</annotation></semantics></math> as free-floating parameters, without ancillary constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A likelihood-ratio test compared the nominal <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CY</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CY}</annotation></semantics></math> hypothesis (<math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LL}}=r_{\mathrm{LM}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LL</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LM</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LL}}=r_{\mathrm{LM}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) against an unconstrained alternative.
A test size of <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upalpha=5\,\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upalpha=5\,\%</annotation></semantics></math> was chosen to limit the rate of false WIMP discovery for several scenarios with reduced <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ECEC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}</annotation></semantics></math> charge yields when not modeled appropriately (<math id="A1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;3\,\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;3\,\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> false WIMP discovery rate below 0.3 %).
After unblinding the data, a p-value of 0.09 was observed, so the nominal <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CY</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CY}</annotation></semantics></math> hypothesis was not rejected.
As a result, the nominal model without charge yield suppression, as described in the main text, was used for the WIMP search.
The best-fit model using only SR1 science data is illustrated in <a href="#A1.F5" title='In A.1 Charge yield of Xe_""^124 double-electron capture events ‣ Appendix A Supplemental Material ‣ WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a>, yielding <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LL}}\approx 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LL</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LL}}\approx 0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LM}}\approx 0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LM</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LM}}\approx 0.8</annotation></semantics></math>.
The best-fit results using the combined SR0+SR1 dataset are <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LL}}=0.80^{+0.08}_{-0.04}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LL</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mn>0.80</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LL}}=0.80^{+0.08}_{-0.04}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LM}}=0.72^{+0.11}_{-0.04}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LM</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mn>0.72</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.11</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LM}}=0.72^{+0.11}_{-0.04}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2502.18005v2/pie_plot_dec_hypothesis_test_cs1_cs2.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/616;" width="476" height="616" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S1: </span>The cS1–cS2 distribution for <math id="A1.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SR1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>SR1</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SR1}</annotation></semantics></math>, from <a href="#S0.F2" title="In WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The model was modified to include the LM+LN and LL <math id="A1.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ECEC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}</annotation></semantics></math> peaks with unconstrained <math id="A1.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CY</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CY}</annotation></semantics></math> while excluding the WIMP component. The regions corresponding to the first (solid gray) and second (hatched gray) steps of unblinding are also indicated.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison, we also report the upper limits using an alternative statistical model with yields as nuisance parameters.
The LM+LN peak yield was loosely constrained to <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LM}}=0.9\pm 0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LM</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LM}}=0.9\pm 0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, based on a conservative extrapolation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.
The LL peak was left unconstrained.
The resulting upper limits are shown in <a href="#A1.F7" title='In A.1 Charge yield of Xe_""^124 double-electron capture events ‣ Appendix A Supplemental Material ‣ WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a> (left), plotted relative to the sensitivity of the nominal model.
At high WIMP masses, the upper limit is a factor of 1.3 more stringent compared to the nominal model.
The sensitivity was computed with a nominal value of <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{LL}}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>LL</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{LL}}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>.
The competing effects of increased background leakage and the added degrees of freedom largely cancel, leaving the sensitivity mostly unchanged.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2502.18005v2/limit_and_sensitivity_ratio.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/445;" width="476" height="445"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S2: </span>Sensitivity and upper limits for the model incorporating reduced <math id="A1.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ECEC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\upnu\mathrm{ECEC}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A1.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CY}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CY</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CY}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters (left) and SR1-only (right), shown relative to the sensitivity of the nominal model for the full SR0+SR1 dataset. The dark (light) shaded region contains <math id="A1.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>68</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math>) of expected upper limits under the background-only hypothesis and the dashed colored lines indicate respective medians.
The solid colored lines represent the observed upper limits for each model. The black lines correspond to the observed upper limit of the nominal model shown in <a href="#S0.F4" title="In WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2502.18005v2/sr0_sr1_number_of_events_sensi_and_ul.svg" id="A1.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/387;" width="476" height="387"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S3: </span>Sensitivity band (yellow-green), median upper limit (dashed line), and observed upper limit (solid line) for the SI SR0+SR1 WIMP search, all expressed in terms of the number of WIMP events.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>SR1-only and WIMP event count limits</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alongside the upper limits derived from the combined SR0+SR1 dataset, we also present the results obtained using only the SR1 dataset.
These are shown in <a href="#A1.F7" title='In A.1 Charge yield of Xe_""^124 double-electron capture events ‣ Appendix A Supplemental Material ‣ WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a> (right) relative to the combined result.
For all masses, the upper limit is above the median, indicating a slight overfluctuation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we present the sensitivity and limits expressed in terms of the corresponding number of WIMP events in <a href="#A1.F7" title='In A.1 Charge yield of Xe_""^124 double-electron capture events ‣ Appendix A Supplemental Material ‣ WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Results on spin-dependent WIMP-nucleon interactions</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the combined dataset in this analysis, we also computed the upper limit on cross sections for spin-dependent (SD) WIMP-nucleon interactions. Since the interaction vanishes for zero nuclear spin, only two xenon isotopes, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 129$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>Xe</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"></mpadded><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"><mn mathsize="0.700em">129</mn></mpadded></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 129$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 131$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>Xe</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"></mpadded><mpadded style="width:12.0pt;" width="12.0pt"><mn mathsize="0.700em">131</mn></mpadded></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle 131$}}_{\makebox[11.95839pt][r]{$\scriptstyle$}}\mathrm{Xe}</annotation></semantics></math>, contribute. The nuclear structure factors used in this analysis are the medians from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. A more recent study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> provides improved results for the SD structure factors which will be used in future analyses. We report limits with respect to the astrophysical parameters recommended in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. The resulting limits are shown in <a href="#A1.F8" title="In A.3 Results on spin-dependent WIMP-nucleon interactions ‣ Appendix A Supplemental Material ‣ WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2502.18005v2/limit_and_sensitivity_sd_neutron.svg" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/387;" width="476" height="387"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2502.18005v2/limit_and_sensitivity_sd_proton.svg" id="A1.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/387;" width="476" height="387"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S4: </span>Upper limits on the spin-dependent WIMP-nucleon cross section, for the “neutron-only” (top) and “proton-only” (bottom) cases. As in <a href="#S0.F4" title="In WIMP Dark Matter Search using a 3.1 Tonne-Year Exposure of the XENONnT Experiment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we show published results from XENONnT using only SR0 data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> (limit recast with a power threshold of 0.16), LZ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and PandaX-4T <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Results from PICO-60 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> are shown in the bottom plot only.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
